% ============================================================================
% comparative-adjective-rule — common English comparative-adjective
% formation rules and what each one actually requires (adj-facts-stdlib,
% LANGUAGE).
% ============================================================================
% Turning an adjective into its comparative form ("smarter", "bigger",
% "drier") is governed by a small set of named rules, each covering a
% different spelling pattern the base adjective falls into — the SAME
% formation-rule SHAPE `superlative-adjective-rule.adj` already ships for
% "-est", but for "-er" instead, a distinct grammar-rule predicate that was
% genuinely uncovered anywhere in this stdlib before this file. Recall is a
% binding query:
%
%     ? comparative_adjective_rule(adjective_ending_in_y, $D)
%       % change_y_to_i_before_er
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `comparative_adjective_rule(rule, description)` carrying the
% source citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query —
% the engine returns the description WITH its source, on the CPU, with
% zero model calls, and abstains on any rule not one of these five
% source-cited examples. The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a description
% and recall which rule it names:
%
%     ? comparative_adjective_rule($R, add_er_suffix)
%       % one_syllable_adjective
%
% NAMING — why this is a NEW predicate (`comparative_adjective_rule`), not
% an extension of either sibling table: `superlative-adjective-rule.adj`
% covers ONLY "-est" formation (confirmed by that table's own header and
% by `suffix-meaning.adj`'s Thread-20 correction note) — a genuinely
% different grammar rule, not this one restated. `suffix-meaning.adj`
% separately ships the AGENTIVE "-er" sense (`_er_agentive` -> "one who;
% person connected with", as in "teacher") — a derivational SUFFIX-MEANING
% fact, categorically different from a comparative-degree GRAMMAR RULE
% about adjective SPELLING. Shipping this as its own new predicate with a
% distinct name, rather than as a bare `_er` row bolted onto either
% existing table, means a recall against `comparative_adjective_rule` can
% never be confused with a recall against `suffix_meaning` or
% `superlative_adjective_rule` — there is no atom-label collision to
% disambiguate in the first place, unlike the `ow`/`ow_long_o` and
% `_er_agentive`/bare-`_er` cases earlier in this stdlib. (`suffix-meaning
% .adj`'s own Thread-19/20 backlog notes flagged exactly this predicate as
% the intended home for comparative `-er`, reserving the bare atom `_er`
% for it since `_er_agentive` is the only `-er`-shaped atom that table
% ships.)
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from Grammarly's "What Are Comparative
% Adjectives? Definition and Examples" article, "5 spelling rules for
% forming comparative adjectives" section, which states each rule
% directly:
%
%     rule (this table's atom label)                    description (this table's atom label)   the row's provenance
%     ---------------------------------------------------  -----------------------------------------  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%     one_syllable_adjective                                add_er_suffix                               `source`: the rule 1 sentence
%     one_syllable_adjective_ending_in_e                    add_r_only                                  `source`: the first rule 2 sentence
%     one_syllable_consonant_vowel_consonant                double_final_consonant_before_er            `cites`: both rule 3 sentences
%     adjective_ending_in_y                                 change_y_to_i_before_er                     `source`: the rule 4 sentence
%     two_syllable_adjective_ending_in_er_ow_or_le          add_er_or_r_without_spelling_change         `cites`: both rule 5 sentences
%
% Each rule's sentences, as the page writes them (the rules mix U+002D
% HYPHEN-MINUS and U+2013 EN DASH, and the page's apostrophe is U+2019; the
% row fields below carry the page's exact characters):
%
%   rule 1  "For most adjectives with one syllable, simply add the suffix –er at the end of the word without changing the spelling."
%   rule 2  "If a one-syllable adjective already ends in -e, just add an -r at the end. You don’t need to add another e."
%   rule 3  "Be careful of one-syllable adjectives with the last three letters in a consonant-vowel-consonant format, like big or thin."
%           "For these, you have to double the last consonant and then add –er."   (a separate paragraph)
%   rule 4  "If an adjective with either one or two syllables ends in a -y, first change the y into an i and then add –er."
%   rule 5  "If an adjective with two syllables ends with -er (like bitter) or –ow (like narrow), you can just add –er to the end without changing the spelling (bitterer or narrower)."
%           "If a two-syllable adjective ends in –le, you can just add –r without adding a second e."   (a separate paragraph)
%
% EACH ROW CARRIES THE PROVENANCE ITS SENTENCES SUPPORT (ADJ-TABLES RS-5e,
% #14986). Rules 1, 2 and 4 each have ONE sentence naming both the spelling
% pattern and the action, so each row takes it as its `source`. Rules 3 and 5
% do not: rule 3's action sentence names its pattern only as "these", and
% rule 5's -er/-ow half and -le half are separate paragraphs, neither stating
% the combined row. Those two rows `cites` both of their sentences, and the
% envelope stays their primary source. The envelope used to be rule 1's own
% sentence, the primary source of all five answers; it is now the article's
% "In theory, any adjective can become a comparative adjective, as long as you
% follow the rules.", which names no rule.
%
% This paragraph used to say the page was "read byte-for-byte" and that all
% five quotes "appear verbatim". Measured 2026-09-15, four quoted sentences did
% not: rule 2 wrote an EN DASH in "add an -r" and a straight apostrophe in
% "don't" (the page has HYPHEN-MINUS and U+2019), and rule 5 wrote HYPHEN-MINUS
% in "-ow" and "-le" (the page has EN DASH). The claim is withdrawn; the quotes
% above are now the page's own characters, each occurring exactly once.
% `trust consensus` —
% Grammarly is a reputable, widely-cited grammar-content company, the same
% tier this stdlib already reserves for other non-.gov language sources
% and the exact same source family `superlative-adjective-rule.adj` itself
% already cites for the sibling "-est" rule set.
%
% A sixth rule the SAME page states — longer adjectives (paraphrased: two
% or more syllables, with the exceptions quoted below) use "more" instead of -er — is
% deliberately NOT a row here,
% for the identical reason `superlative-adjective-rule.adj` excludes its
% own "-est" article's analogous "most" rule: the page's own supporting
% text for it ("Adjectives with two syllables except those that end in
% –er, –ow, –le, or –y", EN DASH before each ending, as the page writes it)
% is a bullet-list fragment, not a clean quotable
% sentence, so a recall on it would require paraphrasing rather than
% quoting. A rule not one of these five — including that "more" rule, and
% any other adjective-comparison behavior — is deliberately NOT a row, so
% a recall ABSTAINS on it rather than inventing a description. (See
% ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table comparative_adjective_rule {
    columns rule, description

    row (one_syllable_adjective, add_er_suffix) {
        source "For most adjectives with one syllable, simply add the suffix –er at the end of the word without changing the spelling."
    }
    row (one_syllable_adjective_ending_in_e, add_r_only) {
        source "If a one-syllable adjective already ends in -e, just add an -r at the end."
    }
    row (one_syllable_consonant_vowel_consonant, double_final_consonant_before_er) {
        cites "Be careful of one-syllable adjectives with the last three letters in a consonant-vowel-consonant format, like big or thin." locator "https://www.grammarly.com/blog/parts-of-speech/comparative-adjectives/"
        cites "For these, you have to double the last consonant and then add –er." locator "https://www.grammarly.com/blog/parts-of-speech/comparative-adjectives/"
    }
    row (adjective_ending_in_y, change_y_to_i_before_er) {
        source "If an adjective with either one or two syllables ends in a -y, first change the y into an i and then add –er."
    }
    row (two_syllable_adjective_ending_in_er_ow_or_le, add_er_or_r_without_spelling_change) {
        cites "If an adjective with two syllables ends with -er (like bitter) or –ow (like narrow), you can just add –er to the end without changing the spelling (bitterer or narrower)." locator "https://www.grammarly.com/blog/parts-of-speech/comparative-adjectives/"
        cites "If a two-syllable adjective ends in –le, you can just add –r without adding a second e." locator "https://www.grammarly.com/blog/parts-of-speech/comparative-adjectives/"
    }

    % THE ENVELOPE WAS RULE 1'S OWN SENTENCE, the primary source of every
    % answer. It is now a framing sentence that names no rule; it stays
    % primary for the two `cites` rows (rules 3 and 5).
    source "In theory, any adjective can become a comparative adjective, as long as you follow the rules."
    locator "https://www.grammarly.com/blog/parts-of-speech/comparative-adjectives/"
    trust consensus
}
